e-Fields-October-4-2026.html#thm:factorization","section":1,"section_title":"Introduction","source":{"current_hash_method":"tex-environment-body-comments-removed","current_statement_sha256":"sha256:0377da5682ac79d8db836164bf231e8de401f7016124cb8ed8f0e92a1d0cd134","file":"preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","line":27,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"aacd3b02d2dacac93e2b0ac3426fbbccc32ce65da3e93d6a7e58c7b0ffd58a79","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:0377da5682ac79d8db836164bf231e8de401f7016124cb8ed8f0e92a1d0cd134","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex#L27"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:factorization}\nThere is a uniform deterministic algorithm that, given a prime $p$ in\nbinary and a nonzero dense polynomial $f\\in\\Fp[x]$ of degree $n$, outputs\nits leading coefficient and its distinct monic irreducible factors with\ntheir multiplicities in\n\\[\n O\\bigl(((n+1)\\lceil\\log_2p\\rceil)^{10^{12}}\\bigr)\n\\]\nbit operations, with absolute implied constant. A constant polynomial\nreturns an empty factor list. The algorithm uses no randomness,\ninteger-factorization oracle, primitive-root oracle, or GRH assumption."},{"appendix":false,"contribution_target":{"fingerprint":"sha256:5c5075f43d9c5ccd1d103a2bc6f1d0a216a83ee1ff5cc67d0f9a642426a0b5f8","grain":"paper-statement","id":"Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026#thm:reduction","title":"thm:reduction"},"credit":{"kind":"producer-declared","license":null,"license_status":"consult-source-not